      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>In Scotland, a Care Inspectorate inspection is not a surprise if you have already run it on yourself. Childcare in Scotland is regulated by the Care Inspectorate, not Ofsted, which covers England only, and not CQC. The Care Inspectorate expects nurseries and other early learning and childcare services to self-evaluate honestly and continuously against its quality framework, so the grades an inspector awards should rarely be a shock to a service that knows itself. The real question is not whether to self-evaluate, it is an expectation, but how to do it so the evaluation is honest, the evidence is captured at the point of observation, and the improvement plan is live rather than a document filed until the next return.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>In Scotland the Care Inspectorate regulates early learning and childcare, not Ofsted, which regulates childcare in England, and not CQC.</li><li>Nurseries are measured against the Health and Social Care Standards and the Care Inspectorate framework How good is our early learning and childcare?, graded on a six-point scale from unsatisfactory to excellent.</li><li>Self-evaluation is an ongoing expectation, not a one-off form: the nursery judges itself honestly against the quality indicators and evidences each judgement.</li><li>An honest self-evaluation finds gaps early and builds evidence; it does not raise the grade. The manager acting on it does.</li><li>Quickler captures the self-evaluation walk, the ratio and safeguarding checks and the premises evidence. It is not a children's records, learning journal or register system.</li><li>Quickler is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Care Inspectorate, and whether your evidence satisfies an inspection is a judgement for you and your inspector.</li></ul>"
